A||||11||camon||Stalled.....and won't start!||||1||
Z||000000||camon||12-05-2001||01:14 PM||||My 87 GT won't start!<P>I was driving home got almost to the house  (while sitting at a red light). Went to pull away from the red light and there was a major hesiation that didn't clear up. I pulled off to the side of the road (all awhile the car was trying to die) well after keeping my foot on the throttle the r's picked up and I drove the last block to my house. Pulled into the parking spot no sooner hit the brakes and it died.<P>Well now it don't start (or even try). It cranks but won't fire!<BR> I checked the #1 spark plug for spark and I can see an arch (don't know if it is strong enough...but there is spark there). While I tried to start it this last time I tried flooding it (by pressing the gas pedal several times) got out and didn't smell any gas. Could this be fuel related? If so what and where is it located. <P>I was thinking maybe fuel pressure regulator (but would this go just like that?????). If you think it is the pressure regulator where is it located and how can I check it!<P>Give me your thoughts on this problem?<P>------------------<BR>   Keith<BR>1987 Fiero GT||||reg||1
Z||000001||84Bill||12-05-2001||01:19 PM||||Not to be a smartass but how much gas you got in it. Fiero fuel guages lie like rugs. <P>BTW pumping the gas will do nothing since Fieros are fuel injected. If yo have a 2.5 pop off the air cleaner and see if the butterfly is wet.||||reg||5
Z||000002||camon||12-05-2001||01:25 PM||||I never knew that (pumping the throttle on fuel injected cars don't flood them) You learn something new everyday!<P>It is a 1987 Fiero GT (V6).<P>BTW....There is well over half a tank of fuel in it (and my gauge has been pretty accurate up to this point)!<P>Well gotta go to work...i'll check back on this thread after work!<P>Thanks guys!<P>------------------<BR>   Keith<BR>1987 Fiero GT||||reg||1
Z||000003||Voytek||12-05-2001||02:59 PM||||Actually (Bill  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> ) fuel injected cars do react to deppressing the pedal.  While cranking, push it half way in, it floods.  Push it all the way in and it cuts off the fuel.  I MAY have it backwards but that's how it works - guaranteed (except for the backwards part  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> )<P>As far as your problem, it sure does sound like a fuel problem.  Perhaps the pump or the relay.  The relay is behind the drivers side firewall (a little black cube).  If you have AC, there are two of them.  They're both identical.  swapping them will tell you if it's the relay.  Otherwise you may be able to get it tested somewhere.<P>If that's not it, and you say you have a spark, I would almost bet on the fuel pump.  Had my pump go in my '85 SE (4 cyl) one time.  The car sputtered a bit.  Then drove again for another couple hundred meters and then died permanently.  Turned out it was the pump.<P>I've seen them die like this a couple of times.<P>Good luck.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/voyteks_sig2.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||camon||12-05-2001||11:41 PM||||Hey thanks for the suggestion! <P>I will check the relays Thursday when I get up (by just replacing them)!<BR>One question concerning them.....What is the easiest way to get them out. Are they in the engine compartment or in the interior?<P>I hope it is as simple as the relays (knowing my luck it ain't)!!!!!!<P>Hate like heck to go through the hassle of the fuel pump!<BR>The tank needs dropped for this...ain't? If so.....Ohhh god!!! I have like half a tank of fuel in there! I do know this past weekend I replaced the fuel filter and it seemed to help a hesitation problem I was having (that is until now). How do I know if the pump is working??? I heard on here that you should be able to hear it (I think) when you turn the key to the on position. Well I never heard anything even before this happened (or maybe I just never noticed it before)!<P>So if it is the pump how hard of a job is it and what needs done!<P>------------------<BR>   Keith<BR>1987 Fiero GT||||reg||1
Z||000005||maryjane||12-06-2001||12:40 AM||||fuel press relay should be on the firewall<BR>in the eng compartment, front left. It is held on with a couple of sheet metal srews or bolts. I think the left one is the fuel pump relay, the right one is the a/c relay.You will only hear the fuel pump run for a few seconds when you first turn the key on. You can take the supply line off that goes to the filter, connect a hose to it, put the other end in a big bottle of some kind and turn on the key. It will tell you if the pump is running, but won't tell you if you are getting the right pressure. Yep, the fuel pump is inside the fuel tank, requires dropping the tank to change it.||||reg||1
Z||000006||camon||12-06-2001||01:00 AM||||Maryjane.....<BR>Thanks! I found them relays.<P>I went out the first time and couldn't see them anywhere (So I assumed they were in the interior). Well after your response I went out and looked again....and low and behold there they are.....right behind the air filter! Thanks!<P>Anyway to check for fuel pressure without needing to jack up the car. My car sits in front of my house and I live along a real busy street (always cars moving). Can it be checked in the engine compartment. <P>If the pump is bad don't know what i'm gonna do to replace it (It won't start so I can't get it off the street) Ohhh I hope it is that relay!<P>Thanks again guys!<BR>Any other input or suggestions are welcome!<P>------------------<BR>   Keith<BR>1987 Fiero GT||||reg||12
Z||000007||tgowens||12-06-2001||10:29 AM||||Keith, to check for fuel pressure there is a Schrader valve on top of the fuel rail - you will see it if you look down right behind the thermostat housing - you can connect a pressure guage there and turn the key on and check your pressure - should have around 37 pounds ........ <P>Honestly, before you start dropping tanks, etc, you might change the ignition module in the distributor - they tend to break down when they heat up and yours might have just gone out.<P>Also, a clogged catalytic comvertor can cause the same symptoms you described.....<P>Also, if you depressed the throttle pedal several times <I>before</I> you turned the key on it would not flood the throttle. The fuel pump will run for about 2 seconds after you turn the key to ignition to pump fuel to the fuel rail to the cold start injector .. you will know if the pump is working by just listining for it. Keep remembering though, these are 15 year old cars and you probably should replace the fuel pump soon anyway.....||||reg||1
Z||000008||camon||12-06-2001||12:59 PM||||Well sorry to say after replacing the fuel pump relay and the other one (the A/C relay I believe) it still won't start nor do I hear a sound when the key is on the on position (that would signal to me the pump has run).<P>TGOWENS:<BR>If the problem were the control module I think I would still be able to hear the fuel pump, would I not??????? Don't want to spend the 26.00 on one only to find out it didn't fix the problem at hand! I'm planning on replacing it sometime soon but want to get it to start first (now if the control module would cause the car not to get fuel then I would buy one today yet).<P>Well I don't have a fuel pressure gauge so the shradder valve test is out. <BR>After installing the relays I sat there and cranked and crancked and cranked the engine. I then pulled plug #1 and it was completely dry...so it ain't getting fuel.<BR>Is there anyway to jump the fuel pump to get it to come on (this way I could determine if it is the pump)? I read in another thread about this but didn't quite understand it!<P>Something else I remember:<BR>Also when it died yesterday (in front of my house) it was sputtering while I had the gas mashed then stalled. I tried to crank it over again and it started but I couldn't get the r's to come up (it was like sputtering then stalled). Well this happened a few times then it just wouldn't start up again.<P>Luckily I have another car so it isn't leaving me carless. But god it has only been a day and I miss driving it already   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>Thank<P>------------------<BR>   Keith<BR>1987 Fiero GT<p>[This message has been edited by camon (edited 12-06-2001).]||||reg||9
